…verified the factual half and it is true of this tree: internal/tui/model.go:541 places event.Output in the non-conversation set, model.go:563 returns the empty phase for it, and no other consumer references event.Output at all. So the comment is honest about current state rather than papering over a live leak. The normative half is the issue. 'no reader-facing path may render directly' is a durable architectural rule with no backing in the graph — the briefing for this file lists exactly one direct invariant and it concerns adversarial-role separation, not output rendering — and nothing enforces it, so a future TUI change can render event.Output with every test still green and no Sensei signal. A rule that lives only in a comment binds nobody. Per CLAUDE.md's knowledge rules, a repair that must never be applied again belongs in the graph, not in prose beside the code it constrains.","correction":"Either record the rule where it can bind — `sensei propose --kind invariant` for the boundary, paired with a forbidden_fix for rendering event.Output directly on a reader-facing path — or reduce the comment to the descriptive half and drop the 'must'/'may not' clauses, so the file does not assert authority it has not been granted."}}
{"id":"20260826T000957.135827271Z-099df706","time":"2026-08-26T00:09:57.135827271Z","session_id":"session-20260825T234902.494846049Z","task_id":"task-1787701742495118225","source":"reviewer","kind":"review.completed","summary":"ACCEPT: The candidate implements exactly the signature and contract the human specified, and I could not break it. internal/agent/agent.go gains only additive code after line 106: Activity trims and passes through any worker whose name is not \"claude\", and routes claude through claudeActivity, which decodes a typed subset of the stream-json envelope and returns \"\" for every non-assistant type, malformed JSON, non-object JSON, empty content, and whitespace-only text. The no-leak claim holds structurally rather than by enumeration: for claude the only values that can ever be returned are block.Text (whitespace-collapsed), block.Name, and the literals \" \", \"using \", \", \", \" (\" and \")\" — no envelope syntax has a path out. encoding/json was already imported at agent.go:6, so nothing was smuggled in. The test table covers all seven categories the intent enumerated. Validation is broker-executed, not worker-reported: gofmt -l, go vet ./..., go build, go test ./... all exit 0. I checked Sensei directly rather than trusting the packet. sensei briefing --file internal/agent/agent.go returns exactly two direct anchors — invariant sensei_code.roles.adversarial_roles_inherit_nothing_and_bind_to_a_revision and failure_mode sensei_code.review_forked_the_conversation_it_was_judging — plus one forbidden fix, sensei_code.give_an_adversarial_role_the_architects_thread_for_context. All three govern reviewer/architect thread separation and place no constraint on output rendering; the briefing lists no required tests for this file. That is consistent with the supplied diff audit (decision: pass, findings_count 0). Scope is clean: two files, no existing line modified, no governance touched, no push or merge, no admission claimed. I did find one real evidence discrepancy and three small defects, none of which would change the diff, so they are recorded rather than blocking.  (reviewer · via claude · session fresh · candidate ccad0517db9b)","payload":{"provenance":{"task_id":"task-1787701742495118225","role":"reviewer","provider":"claude","session_id":"session-20260825T234902.494846049Z","session_mode":"fresh","base_sha":"da311a56277119d4def7bc7526aadf119e7f96ad","candidate_digest":"ccad0517db9b8020","graph_build_commit":"96f19456f5fb","at":"2026-08-26T00:09:57.135376464Z"},"decision":"accept","summary":"The candidate implements exactly the signature and contract the human specified, and I could not break it. internal/agent/agent.go gains only additive code after line 106: Activity trims and passes through any worker whose name is not \"claude\", and routes claude through claudeActivity, which decodes a typed subset of the stream-json envelope and returns \"\" for every non-assistant type, malformed JSON, non-object JSON, empty content, and whitespace-only text. The no-leak claim holds structurally rather than by enumeration: for claude the only values that can ever be returned are block.Text (whitespace-collapsed), block.Name, and the literals \" \", \"using \", \", \", \" (\" and \")\" — no envelope syntax has a path out. encoding/json was already imported at agent.go:6, so nothing was smuggled in. The test table covers all seven categories the intent enumerated. Validation is broker-executed, not worker-reported: gofmt -l, go vet ./..., go build, go test ./... all exit 0. I checked Sensei directly rather than trusting the packet. sensei briefing --file internal/agent/agent.go returns exactly two direct anchors — invariant sensei_code.roles.adversarial_roles_inherit_nothing_and_bind_to_a_revision and failure_mode sensei_code.review_forked_the_conversation_it_was_judging — plus one forbidden fix, sensei_code.give_an_adversarial_role_the_architects_thread_for_context. All three govern reviewer/architect thread separation and place no constraint on output rendering; the briefing lists no required tests for this file. That is consistent with the supplied diff audit (decision: pass, findings_count 0). Scope is clean: two files, no existing line modified, no governance touched, no push or merge, no admission claimed. I did find one real evidence discrepancy and three small defects, none of which would change the diff, so they are recorded rather than blocking.","instructions":"No repair is required for this revision. Carry f2 and f3 into the next change that touches Activity, and either discharge or drop the normative clause in f4. For f1, attach the file-narrowed preflight and briefing to future candidates touching internal/agent/agent.go — I ran them for this one, so no work is outstanding.","findings":[{"id":"f1","severity":"major","claim":"The packet's Sensei evidence asserts that Sensei holds essentially nothing about this change: 'status: PREFLIGHT_STATUS_EMPTY, risk_class: UNKNOWN_IMPACT, confidence: CONFIDENCE_LOW, coverage: anchors=0 files=0 indexed=0 sufficient=false'.","reference":"SENSEI PREFLIGHT block in the review packet, versus `sensei preflight --file internal/agent/agent.go` and `sensei briefing --file internal/agent/agent.go` run in this session","reason":"That preflight is the task-only form, and its own required_actions field says 'Coverage is thin — read the surrounding code, then re-run preflight with --file to narrow'. That instruction was never discharged, and the packet contains no briefing at all. When I narrow it as Sensei asks, the answer changes materially: 'Status: PREFLIGHT_STATUS_OK   Risk: ARCHITECTURE_SENSITIVE   Confidence: CONFIDENCE_MEDIUM' and 'Coverage: sufficient=true anchors=2 (2 direct anchor(s) matched)', with a required action 'Re-read CLAUDE.md hard rules; call awareness.briefing(file=...) per the rule R2 contract'. So the evidence bound to this candidate understates the file's risk class and skips the briefing CLAUDE.md Rule 1 requires for a protected file — and internal/agent/agent.go is protected, since the briefing returns direct invariants for it. Two further signals show why the task-level view came back empty: `sensei protection-status` reports 'snapshot: stale — the repository changed since it was last published; re-run `sensei bootstrap`', and the briefing's code_symbol index already lists 'internal/agent/agent.go:Activity' even though the base tree at da311a5 does not define it (`git log -S'func Activity' --all` shows it on sibling branches f3d9891, d86a9a5, 01f3fe1). The graph's symbol coverage for this file is therefore not bound to the revision under review. This does not block, because I ran both narrowed calls myself and the two anchors that surface are the adversarial-role invariant and failure mode, which constrain how I review rather than what this diff does — a revise cycle would return a byte-identical diff.","correction":"For any future candidate touching internal/agent/agent.go, run and attach `sensei preflight --file internal/agent/agent.go` and `sensei briefing --file internal/agent/agent.go` rather than the task-only preflight, and re-run `sensei bootstrap` so the published snapshot stops trailing the working tree.","proof_gap":"The packet proves that Sensei was asked a question too broad to answer, not that Sensei had nothing to say about the file being edited. I closed that gap by asking the narrowed question directly; the packet as delivered did not."},{"id":"f2","severity":"minor","claim":"Intent item 2 asserts that 'every successful Claude rendering is a single readable line'.","reference":"internal/agent/agent.go, claudeActivity, the `case \"tool_use\"` branch","reason":"The prose path defends the single-line property explicitly — `strings.Join(strings.Fields(block.Text), \" \")`, with a comment saying Fields 'collapses the newlines an assistant writes freely, so a multi-line answer stays one activity line'. The tool path uses only `strings.TrimSpace(block.Name)`, which trims the edges and leaves interior whitespace intact. A tool name carrying an embedded newline would produce a multi-line activity line, so the single-line invariant is enforced on one of the two value sources and merely assumed on the other. Real tool names are identifiers, so this is a consistency defect rather than an observed break — but the asymmetry is exactly where the invariant would fail if it ever failed.","correction":"Normalize the tool name the same way the prose is normalized: `strings.Join(strings.Fields(block.Name), \" \")`, so both value sources into the rendered line are held to the single-line property by construction rather than by assumption."},{"id":"f3","severity":"minor","claim":"The test asserts a general no-leak property with `if c.worker == \"claude\" \u0026\u0026 strings.ContainsAny(got, \"{}[]\\\"\")` and the message 'leaked envelope syntax to the reader'.","reference":"internal/agent/agent_test.go, TestActivityShowsWorkAndNeverProtocol, the assertion following the got != c.want check","reason":"The guard is unreachable as written. It runs only after `got != c.want` has already failed the test, so it can only ever be evaluated when got equals want — and every claude case's want is either \"\" or brace-free prose chosen by the author. The check therefore asserts a property of the hand-written expectation, not of the function, and cannot fail for any table this table could contain. It reads as a standing guarantee over arbitrary input while proving nothing beyond the enumeration above it. The underlying claim is still true — it is carried by the implementation's structure, as noted in the summary — but this assertion is not what carries it.","correction":"Either drop the guard as decoration, or make it earn its keep by running the same containment check over a corpus of inputs with no fixed expectations — truncated envelopes, arbitrary byte strings, deeply nested JSON — asserting only that Activity(\"claude\", x) is either empty or free of envelope punctuation. That form can fail, and so would prove the universal claim the current guard only appears to make.","proof_gap":"The 'no raw envelope syntax reaches the reader' property is proven by enumeration of sixteen chosen lines plus a structural reading of the code, not by any assertion that could fail on an unenumerated input."},{"id":"f4","severity":"minor","claim":"The doc comment states a normative rule: 'Activity is the boundary a reader-facing path must pass claude output through; CLI.Run still emits raw stdout lines as event.Output, which no reader-facing path may render directly.'","reference":"internal/agent/agent.go, the doc comment above func Activity","reason":"I verified the factual half and it is true of this tree: internal/tui/model.go:541 places event.Output in the non-conversation set, model.go:563 returns the empty phase for it, and no other consumer references event.Output at all. So the comment is honest about current state rather than papering over a live leak. The normative half is the issue. 'no reader-facing path may render directly' is a durable architectural rule with no backing in the graph — the briefing for this file lists exactly one direct invariant and it concerns adversarial-role separation, not output rendering — and nothing enforces it, so a future TUI change can render event.Output with every test still green and no Sensei signal. A rule that lives only in a comment binds nobody. Per CLAUDE.md's knowledge rules, a repair that must never be applied again belongs in the graph, not in prose beside the code it constrains.","correction":"Either record the rule where it can bind — `sensei propose --kind invariant` for the boundary, paired with a forbidden_fix for rendering event.Output directly on a reader-facing path — or reduce the comment to the descriptive half and drop the 'must'/'may not' clauses, so the file does not assert authority it has not been granted."}]}}
{"id":"20260826T001000.431773760Z-b2f96628","time":"2026-08-26T00:10:00.43177376Z","session_id":"session-20260825T234902.494846049Z","task_id":"task-1787701742495118225","source":"sensei","kind":"change.reported","summary":"Architectural change report\n  for: internal/agent streams a worker's raw output verbatim. For the claude worker that output is JSON envelopes, so the reader is shown protocol rather than activity.\n\nAdd the exported function:\n\n    func Activity(name, line string) string\n\nFor a worker whose name is not \"claude\" it returns the line trimmed of surrounding whitespace and otherwise unchanged. For \"claude\" it renders one readable activity line from an assistant envelope -- representing the assistant's text, and naming any tool being used -- and returns the empty string for anything that is not assistant activity, including envelopes of other types, empty content, and lines that are not valid JSON. No raw envelope syntax may reach the reader.\n\n  files: 1 added, 1 modified, 0 deleted\n  go: 1 source, 1 test\n\n  governed by:\n    An adversarial role inherits nothing from the work it judges, and its verdict is bound to the revision it read\n\n  Sensei risk class: ARCHITECTURE_SENSITIVE\n\n  Sensei diff audit:\n    schema: awareness.diff_audit/v1\n    decision: pass\n    availability: available\n    input_trust: caller_supplied\n    digest: 200559d5c1526331dca1aa350b2e7e40b2a5772a8486bf3a858c9428fe2495c0\n    changed_files_count: 2\n    findings_count: 0\n    \n    No blocking findings found in supplied diff.\n\n  not established by this report:\n    - admission: not requested, so this candidate is not admitted\n    - correctness: reviewer acceptance is not proof, and passing tests are not proof","payload":{"files":[{"path":"internal/agent/agent.go","status":"modified"},{"path":"internal/agent/agent_test.go","status":"added"}],"audit":"schema: awareness.diff_audit/v1\ndecision: pass\navailability: available\ninput_trust: caller_supplied\ndigest: 200559d5c1526331dca1aa350b2e7e40b2a5772a8486bf3a858c9428fe2495c0\nchanged_files_count: 2\nfindings_count: 0\n\nNo blocking findings found in supplied diff.","risk":"ARCHITECTURE_SENSITIVE","governing":["An adversarial role inherits nothing from the work it judges, and its verdict is bound to the revision it read"]}}
